{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role in the history of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem which gave him strong ties to the royal family of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His early life involved captivity after his father was murdered by the Assassins yet he eventually secured his release and assumed control over his county. Raymond became known for his diplomatic skills and his deep understanding of Islamic culture which set him apart from many other Frankish nobles of his time.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond faced immense danger during his youth when his father fell victim to a political assassination plot orchestrated by the Order of Assassins. Young Raymond spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo where he learned Arabic and developed a profound respect for Muslim customs and leadership styles. This unique education shaped his future policies and allowed him to negotiate peace treaties with neighboring Muslim rulers more effectively than his peers could ever hope to achieve. His time in captivity hardened his resolve while also broadening his perspective on the complex geopolitical landscape of the Levant.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance during certain periods of his reign. Raymond sought to maintain stability within the kingdom by advocating for peaceful coexistence with Saladin rather than engaging in reckless military campaigns. He married Eschiva of Bures and thereby gained control over the Principality of Galilee which increased his power significantly. Internal conflicts arose between Raymond and Guy of Lusignan because the latter favored aggressive warfare that threatened the fragile peace Raymond had worked so hard to build. These tensions eventually led to a civil war within the Crusader states that weakened their overall defensive capabilities against external threats.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\nRaymond died in 1187 shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army suffered a catastrophic defeat at the hands of Saladin. Some historians argue that his absence or inability to unify the Christian forces contributed directly to this military collapse. He left no legitimate heirs so his county passed to a distant relative while his political faction dissolved soon after his passing. His life represents a fascinating study of a Christian noble who embraced Eastern traditions and sought diplomacy over constant warfare in a region defined by religious conflict. The loss of his wise leadership marked the beginning of the end for the first Kingdom of Jerusalem as it existed in its original form.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -3.576285e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0020087855}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.01634175}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.04692668}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.00059860974}, {